# Template for `.dev.vars` (gitignored). Copy this file, keep the copy off GitHub.
#
# The Deploy to Cloudflare button reads THIS file to decide what to ask the new owner for
# during deployment. Every uncommented key below becomes a prompt — so there is exactly one,
# and it is a word a person can invent.
#
# Anything you add here, someone deploying this template has to answer. Keep it to one line.
#
# LEAVE THE VALUES EMPTY. The button does not only read the KEYS — it pre-fills the VALUE
# into a masked field the deployer cannot read. A placeholder here therefore becomes the
# real secret of every site deployed from this repository, published, in a public file.
# `SETUP_CODE="pick-any-word-you-like"` shipped for one evening and did exactly that.
# An empty value leaves the field visibly blank, which is the only state a person can check.

# A word you make up, typed while deploying. It gates the one-time claim, so that nobody who
# finds the URL before you can open the site and set the password first.
#
# Type your own — anything unguessable, `blue-kettle-rain` is the right shape — and write it
# down until the site is claimed: the deploy form masks it, so you cannot read it back.
# Leaving it blank is allowed and means NO gate: whoever opens the site first owns it.
#
# It is not the admin password. You choose that in the browser, afterwards, and this stops
# mattering the moment you do.
SETUP_CODE=


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Everything below is OPTIONAL and deliberately commented out.
#
# The site normally gets its password from the browser: open it after deploying and it asks
# you to choose one. These two variables are the older, manual route — still supported, and
# still winning over the database when both exist — but they require generating a PBKDF2
# hash, which is not something the site's owner should ever have to do.
#
# If you do want them, `npm run hash-password` prints both values.
#
# SINGLE QUOTES, not double. The hash contains `$`, which a shell expands: pasted into zsh
# with double quotes, `pbkdf2$100000$SALT$DIGEST` silently becomes the six characters
# `pbkdf2`, and then no password on earth logs you in. This cost an evening once.
#
# ADMIN_PASSWORD_HASH='pbkdf2$100000$SALT_GOES_HERE$DIGEST_GOES_HERE'
# TOKEN_SECRET='any-long-random-string-rotating-it-logs-out-live-sessions'
